WHY IT IS A DEFECT AND NOT AN INCONVENIENCE (§3 step 1):
  The ADRs normatively name roughly 490 reason codes. The registry carried 201.
  Every implementation domain was therefore forced to substitute, and the
  substitutions changed meaning rather than merely losing detail:
    - PLATFORM.PRIV.CLIENT_UNAUTHORIZED -> POLICY.POLICY_DENIED discards
      ADR-0017 §11.12's dedicated exit code and tells a correct script to stop
      retrying something a group-membership change would fix.
    - MGMT.RESYNC_REQUIRED -> MGMT.STREAM_COMPACTED makes MI-9a's two distinct
      conditions indistinguishable at the exact point a client must tell them
      apart.
    - Five distinct Windows start-sequence refusals arrived as
      PLATFORM.ADAPTER_UNAVAILABLE, so a fleet query could not count the hosts
      whose boot window is unprotected — the one number KS-19 exists to make
      countable.
  ADR-0011 §11.9's endpoint list is the second half: without it no platform
  could install the DoH containment that section requires of WFP, pf AND
  nftables, so a browser with a pinned DoH endpoint resolved off-tunnel on
  every desktop.

WHY IT IS COMPATIBLE (§3 step 5):
  Reason codes travel the wire as STRINGS. Adding entries is a pure superset: a
  reader that meets a new code handles it exactly as it already handles an
  unknown one, using the carried attributes per ADR-0015 rule 5. No field
  number, no enum value, no wire format, and no generated binding moved. The
  closed set of sixteen domains is unchanged. The one non-additive edit —
  max_name_bytes 24 -> 32 — RELAXES a validator bound that was rejecting a
  Phase-1-mandated 27-byte token, and was already required by CF-6's amendment
  to ADR-0014 N-11.

WHAT IT COST, RECORDED:
  Raising max_name_bytes exposed that the per-field capability bounds are not
  mutually consistent with max_advertisement_bytes: 32 tokens at the name cap
  is ~1216 B against a declared 512 B advertisement cap. That was ALREADY true
  at 24 bytes (~960 B); the old wire test compared the unreachable product
  against the 1200 B C4 datagram instead and passed by coincidence. The test is
  rewritten to assert what a receiver actually enforces, and to assert that the
  byte cap is load-bearing.

